---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const fig = (n: string) => `${base}/figures/${n}`
const vid = (n: string) => `${base}/videos/${n}`

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Minkyu Kang', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
	{ name: 'JinHo Kim' },
	{ name: 'Eunseop Shin' },
	{ name: 'Dayeon Hwang' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
	{ label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA' },
	{ label: 'Slides', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA/blob/main/README.md' },
]
---

<Layout
	title='Mean Collapse is a Serious Bottleneck of VLA'
	pageTitle='Mean Collapse in VLA'
	description='Even with the privileged top-view (MDP) input, a Qwen3-VL VLA trained to act on the latest observation only exhibits mean collapse — moving erratically and falling over. We propose a memory-based steering module on the frozen VLA as a fix.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	hero={fig('ant_frame_4x.png')}
	heroAlt='Ant-Maze rollout — top-view (MDP) and 3rd-person (POMDP) frames'
	faviconHref={`${base}/weasel-logo.png`}
>
	<div class='highlight-box'>
		<h2>TL;DR</h2>
		<p>
			A VLA trained to act on the <b>latest observation only</b> — even with the
			<b>privileged top-view</b> input that turns the task into an MDP — still exhibits
			<b>mean collapse</b>: the agent moves erratically, walks in place, falls over.
			On visually aliased states, the optimal Markovian action is the
			<b>mean over plausible futures</b>, and that mean is often a degenerate motion.
		</p>
		<p>
			Counterintuitively, the <b>3rd-person view (POMDP)</b> rollouts in our setup
			explored the maze more aggressively and <b>opportunistically</b> turned into the
			pink goal whenever it came into view — but on harder seeds the same Markovian
			policy gets stuck for tens of seconds, frozen in place by collapse.
		</p>
		<p>
			Our take: <b>Memory + memory-based steering on a frozen VLA</b> is the
			right kind of fix. We design a <b>GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver</b> steering module
			that builds a fixed-size memory from past ViT features and gates it back into
			the frozen base VLA — without retraining the base model.
		</p>
		<p>
			The same pathology shows up on a real GUI agent
			(<a href='https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero'>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>) running
			a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz: solved and fresh tiles look
			almost identical, the agent has no memory, and it loops.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h1>1. Background — MDP vs. POMDP</h1>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('mdp_vs_pomdp.png')} alt='MDP vs POMDP graphical models' />
		<figcaption>
			MDP: the agent acts on the state <i>s</i> directly. POMDP: the agent only sees
			observations <i>o</i> and must maintain a belief <i>b</i> over the latent state.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>MDP.</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>): the agent sees the
			task-relevant state directly.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>POMDP.</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>) where
			<i>h<sub>t</sub></i> = (<i>o<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>0</sub>, …, o<sub>t</sub></i>): only
			noisy/partial observations are available, so the policy must condition on history.
		</li>
		<li>
			A Markovian policy on <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> is <b>suboptimal</b> when
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>2. Most VLA Benchmarks Hide the POMDP</h1>

	<p>
		Most VLA benchmarks are effectively MDPs: the current scene already encodes the
		task-relevant state, so a policy reading only the latest observation looks fine.
		LIBERO and SIMPLER-style manipulation are like that — the relevant scene is on the
		screen at every timestep.
	</p>
	<p>
		GUI agents (UI-TARS-style) are typically deployed the same way: each action is
		decided from a <b>summary plus the current screenshot only</b>. Both setups
		assume <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>) suffices — i.e. Markovian
		on the latest observation.
	</p>

	<h1>3. But Real Tasks Are POMDPs</h1>

	<p>
		Crafting a pickaxe in Minecraft requires the fact &ldquo;I already chopped a tree&rdquo;
		— and that fact is not in the current screen. Inventory, past actions, and
		previously visited rooms are <b>hidden state</b>. Real long-horizon tasks
		(Minecraft, GUI agents, manipulation under occlusion) are POMDPs: the policy needs
		<b>history</b> <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>, not just <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>.
	</p>

	<h1>4. Setup — A Controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant Maze</h1>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('maze_setup.png')} alt='OGBench Ant-Maze setup with two views' />
		<figcaption>
			We build a controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant Maze. The agent must reach a
			pink goal from a fixed start. We can independently choose the camera given to
			the policy.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('two_views.png')} alt='Same world, two views — visual aliasing' />
		<figcaption>
			<b>Top-view (MDP).</b> <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> = <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>: position, goal,
			full maze layout. <b>3rd-person view (POMDP).</b>
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ∼ Ω(· | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>): only what is in front of the
			ant — visually similar 3rd-person frames can correspond to <b>different</b>
			next steps. This is the source of mean collapse.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>5. Method — Ant VLA, OpenVLA-OFT Style</h1>

	<p>
		Our base policy is a Vision-Language-Action model in the
		<a href='https://openvla-oft.github.io/'>OpenVLA-OFT</a> style with a
		<a href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631'>Qwen3-VL 2B</a> backbone.
		Both the ViT and the LLM decode head are fine-tuned with <b>LoRA</b> on the Ant-Maze
		demonstrations. Input is an image observation (top-view <i>or</i> 3rd-person), Ant
		proprioception, and a <code>Go to the point ⟨x, y⟩</code> instruction.
		Output is a <b>parallel-decoded action chunk</b> in OpenVLA-OFT style.
	</p>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('ant_vla_training.png')} alt='Ant VLA training pipeline' />
		<figcaption>
			Training pipeline. Image + proprio + instruction → Qwen3-VL 2B (LoRA on ViT and
			LLM head) → parallel-decoded action chunk. We train one VLA per camera (top-view
			vs. 3rd-person) so that the only difference between settings is observability.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('qwen3vl_arch.png')} alt='Qwen3-VL backbone architecture' />
		<figcaption>
			Qwen3-VL backbone, with the ViT depth-tap points and the action head we adopt
			from OpenVLA-OFT. Diagram reused from the DASH report.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>6. What We Observe — Even Top-View Trained Qwen3-VL Mean-Collapses</h1>

	<p>
		We expected the top-view (MDP) policy to be a clean upper bound. It is not. The
		Qwen3-VL VLA trained to act on the latest observation only — <i>even when that
		observation is the privileged top-view</i> — still <b>mean-collapses</b>. The ant
		walks in place, twitches, drifts sideways, and frequently <b>falls over</b>.
	</p>

	<p>
		Counterintuitively, the <b>3rd-person view (POMDP)</b> rollouts in our setup
		actually moved more — the policy explored the maze more aggressively and, when
		the pink goal happened to come into view, it <b>opportunistically</b> turned and
		dove into it. So we got some wins on the partially observed setup that the
		fully observed setup struggled to produce.
	</p>

	<p>
		But on the <b>harder seeds</b> the same Markovian policy enters a deep mean-collapse
		state and barely moves at all — for tens of seconds.
	</p>

	<h2>6.1 Opportunistic successes (3rd-person POMDP)</h2>

	<div class='video-grid'>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_00.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>
				3rd-person, no memory. Agent wanders, sees the pink goal, opportunistically
				turns in.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_03.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>
				3rd-person, no memory. Same opportunistic pattern on a different seed.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<h2>6.2 Mean-collapse failure modes</h2>

	<div class='video-grid'>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_falling.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>
				Falling-over collapse — the &ldquo;mean&rdquo; action is degenerate gait, the
				ant tips and resets repeatedly.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_loop.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>
				Looping collapse — the agent re-enters the same aliased corner and
				cycles between the same two actions.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<h2>6.3 Extreme stuck case — <code>Mean_Collapse_After_40</code></h2>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('mean_collapse_after_40.mp4')}></video>
		<figcaption>
			Extreme mean collapse. After step ~40 the policy stops moving almost entirely
			— the action distribution has collapsed to a near-zero mean and the ant is
			frozen in place for the remainder of the rollout.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>7. Proposed Fix — A Memory-Based Steering Module on the Frozen VLA</h1>

	<p>
		Our fix is a small <b>memory-based steering module</b> bolted onto a
		<b>frozen</b> base VLA. It (i) builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT
		features using a <b>GLA Linear-SSM</b> temporal mixer + <b>Perceiver</b>
		compression, then (ii) injects <i>Z</i> back into the frozen ViT at intermediate
		depths and adds a small residual to the action head. The base VLA is never
		re-trained.
	</p>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('steering_module.png')} alt='Steering module added to the frozen VLA' />
		<figcaption>
			Learnable steering (peach) builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT
			features via a <b>GLA temporal mixer (Linear SSM)</b> + <b>Perceiver
			compression</b>, then injects it into the frozen ViT at L8 / L16 and into the
			action head through gated residuals. The <b>frozen base VLA (green) is never
			re-trained</b>.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h2>7.1 Formulation</h2>

	<p>
		Past ViT features <i>h<sup>(ℓ)</sup><sub>t−k</sub></i> at depths
		ℓ ∈ &#123;8, 16&#125;, k = 1, …, K:
	</p>

	<pre class='codebox'><code>{`(1) Linear SSM mix — fixed-size state, content-gated decay:
    S_k = σ(g_k) ⊙ S_{k-1} + K_k^T V_k
    O_k = Q_k S_k

(2) Compress to m = 32 memory tokens (Perceiver cross-attention):
    Z^(ℓ) = CrossAttn(Q_learn, O_{1:K}^(ℓ))   ∈ R^{32×d}

(3) Gated residual injection into frozen ViT:
    h~_t^(ℓ) = h_t^(ℓ) + σ(α_ℓ) · CrossAttn(h_t^(ℓ), Z^(ℓ))

(4) Residual correction on frozen action head:
    a_t = π_base(o_t)  +  σ(α_res) · Δ(Z^(16))
                  ↑frozen↑`}</code></pre>

	<h2>7.2 Why a State-Space Model?</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>Long token sequence.</b> Flattened past is
			<i>T = K · N<sub>patch</sub></i> ∼ 2000 tokens. Quadratic attention is
			<i>O(T²)</i>; a Linear SSM is <i>O(T)</i> with a fixed-size state <i>S</i>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Content-gated decay.</b> σ(<i>g<sub>t</sub></i>) learns a per-token forget — more
			expressive than RetNet&apos;s fixed γ, more stable than plain linear attention.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Length extrapolation.</b> Train with <i>K</i> ∼ 𝒰[1, 32] + sinusoidal
			temporal PE → robust at <i>K</i> = (64, 128) at inference.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Identity-init gates.</b> σ(α) = 0 → the module starts as a no-op, recovers
			the base VLA exactly, then learns corrections safely.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>8. Bonus — Same Pathology on a GUI Agent</h1>

	<p>
		The exact same Markovian-on-<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> failure shows up in a real GUI
		agent. <a href='https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero'>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>,
		a VLM-based agent, runs on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
		Solved pairs turn slightly darker, but solved and fresh tiles still look almost
		identical — and the agent has no memory of which ones it already tapped.
	</p>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('gui_loop.png')} alt='GUI agent loop case on Duolingo' />
		<figcaption>
			The agent re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it, and the screen returns to
			the same state — <i>C → E → C → E …</i>. Same pathology as the Ant POMDP:
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> (screenshot) ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i> (solved-set).
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('gui_rollout.mp4')}></video>
		<figcaption>GUI agent rollout (sped up) — getting stuck in the loop.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>9. Conclusion</h1>

	<ul>
		<li>
			A Markovian VLA on <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> mean-collapses
			<b>even when given the privileged top-view (MDP) input</b> — degenerate gait,
			falling over, frozen agents.
		</li>
		<li>
			3rd-person (POMDP) is not strictly worse in our setup; it explores more and can
			get opportunistic wins. But on hard seeds it lands in the same collapse
			(see <code>Mean_Collapse_After_40</code>).
		</li>
		<li>
			The right shape of the fix is <b>memory + memory-based steering on a frozen
			VLA</b>: a Linear-SSM temporal mixer, Perceiver compression, gated residual
			injection — without re-training the base policy.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<hr />
